  "@id": "https://openminds.om-i.org/instances/molecularEntity/Alpha-Synuclein",
  "@type": "https://openminds.om-i.org/types/MolecularEntity",
  "definition": "A small, intrinsically disordered presynaptic protein that acts as a non-classical molecular chaperone, facilitating the folding and assembly of diverse protein complexes to govern synaptic vesicle trafficking, neurotransmitter release, and cellular homeostasis.",
  "description": "Encoded by the SNCA gene and abundantly expressed in the brain, Alpha-synuclein lacks a rigid three-dimensional structure in the cytoplasm, acting as a conformational chameleon that dynamically shifts shape depending on its local cellular environment and binding partners. When interacting with curved lipid membranes or specific target proteins, it adopts structured conformations that stabilize multiprotein assemblies and protect vulnerable cellular machinery against stress-induced denaturation. Through this versatile, chaperone-like behavior, it coordinates a broad spectrum of physiological processes, from promoting SNARE-complex assembly during synaptic plasticity to acting as a histone chaperone in nuclear regulation. However, the very structural flexibility that enables these diverse functions also leaves the protein susceptible to misfolding when disrupted by SNCA mutations, cellular stress, or aging. Under these pathological conditions, alpha-synuclein self-associates into toxic oligomers and insoluble amyloid fibrils, driving the progressive neurodegeneration characteristic of synucleinopathies such as Parkinson's disease.",
  "name": "Alpha-Synuclein",
